\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Results for finding the relationship between $F_{620}$ and $l_0$. (\textbf {a}) $F_{620_{10}}$ as a function of $l_0$, at $P_{620}$. Dashed line is the $F_{620_{10}}$ data from Festo. Solid line is the fit from Equation (\ref {eq:Fmax10}). (\textbf {b}) $F_{620_{20}}$ as a function of $l_0$ at $P_{620}$. Dashed line is $F_{620_{20}}$ data from Festo. Solid line is the fit from Equation (\ref {eq:Fmax20}).}}{46}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline {(\normalfont (\textbf {a}))}{\ignorespaces {\centering }}}{46}{subfigure.2.1}%
\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline {(\normalfont (\textbf {b}))}{\ignorespaces {\centering }}}{46}{subfigure.2.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Results for finding the relationship between $F_{620}$, $l_0$, $P_{620}$, and $\epsilon _{620_{10}}$. (\textbf {a}) Isoclines of the surface fit for $F_{620_{10}}(l_0,P_{620})$. (\textbf {b}) $\epsilon _{620_{10}}$ versus $l_0$ at $P_{620}$. Although there is a general trend of longer resting lengths producing more contraction, no conclusive relationship between $\epsilon _{620}$ and $l_0$ could be deduced from this experiment. The error bars show the effect of being $\pm $\qty {1}{\mm } in measuring $l_0$ and $l_{620}$.}}{50}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline {(\normalfont (\textbf {a}))}{\ignorespaces {\centering }}}{50}{subfigure.3.1}%
\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline {(\normalfont (\textbf {b}))}{\ignorespaces {\centering }}}{50}{subfigure.3.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Surface fit for $F^*(\epsilon ^*,P^*)$. Solid lines are from our model. Dashed lines from Festo supplied data. In total, 537 data points were collected and used to fit the 3D surfaces (321 data points for $\diameter $\qty {10}{\mm } BPAs and 216 data points for $\diameter $\qty {20}{\mm } BPAs). In total, 80\% of these data were used for training, and 20\% were used for validation. For figure clarity, not all data is included and plotted circles represent collected data at $\pm \qty {10}{\kPa }$ of the stated pressure. (\textbf {a}) Fit data for $\diameter $\qty {10}{\mm } Festo BPAs. (\textbf {b}) Fit data for $\diameter $\qty {20}{\mm } Festo BPAs.}}{51}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline {(\normalfont (\textbf {a}))}{\ignorespaces {\centering }}}{51}{subfigure.4.1}%
\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline {(\normalfont (\textbf {b}))}{\ignorespaces {\centering }}}{51}{subfigure.4.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Comparison between measured and predicted BPA force at varying amounts of contraction and pressure. A $y=x$ perfect fit line is added for reference. The BPAs compared are: (\textbf {a}) $\diameter $\qty {10}{\mm }, $l_0=\qty {257}{\mm }$, (\textbf {b}) $\diameter $\qty {10}{\mm }, $l_0=\qty {233}{\mm }$, (\textbf {c}) $\diameter $\qty {20}{\mm }, $l_0=\qty {300}{\mm }$, and (\textbf {d}) $\diameter $\qty {20}{\mm }, $l_0=\qty {450}{\mm }$.}}{57}{figure.caption.24}%
